# 3. CONTROLS AND INPUT DESIGN

The game must work using a mouse and keyboard on desktop and touch gestures on Android browsers.

## 3.1 Mobile controls

The game should use a responsive landscape-first layout with an optional portrait-friendly interface if practical.

| Gesture | Action |
|---|---|
| Swipe left | Move left or dodge left |
| Swipe right | Move right or dodge right |
| Swipe up | Jump, climb or perform the context-sensitive upward action |
| Swipe down | Slide or duck |
| Tap | Interact, grab, activate or attack when indicated |
| Long press | Special action only when explicitly shown |

Context determines the exact action. For example, an upward swipe during a balloon encounter can grab the rope rather than trigger an ordinary jump.

Avoid requiring multi-finger gestures during the initial release.

## 3.2 Desktop controls

- A / Left Arrow: move left.
- D / Right Arrow: move right.
- W / Up Arrow / Space: jump or contextual upward action.
- S / Down Arrow: slide.
- Left Mouse Button: tap/interact.
- Escape: pause or close an open menu.

Controls should be configurable in a later settings update.

## 3.3 Input handling rules

- Use pointer events for mouse and touch compatibility.
- Prevent accidental page scrolling during active gameplay.
- Distinguish taps from swipes using a configurable movement threshold.
- Consume one input once, preventing duplicate actions.
- Ignore gameplay input while menus, settings or pause screens are open.
- Disable repeated actions until the current action reaches a valid state.
- Provide a short visual confirmation for every accepted input.
- Support browser focus loss and tab switching without accidentally registering controls.

Never require pixel-perfect swipes.

