# 20. MASTER DEVELOPMENT PROMPT

Use the following prompt with an AI coding agent to develop the actual game.

## Role

You are a senior browser-game developer, 3D gameplay programmer, game designer, technical artist and QA engineer.

Build a complete, playable 3D browser game using the specifications in this document. Treat the GDD as the source of truth. You are responsible for implementing the actual game, not merely creating a design mockup or a visual demonstration.

## Primary objective

Create a polished stylised 3D endless chase game in which a masked cartoon thief escapes from police through an evolving city, performs theft interactions, collects coins during dedicated bonus phases, unlocks temporary gadgets and progresses through foot, motorcycle, car and hot-air-balloon escape stages.

The game must run in desktop browsers and Android mobile browsers.

## Technical requirements

1. Use Three.js, TypeScript and Vite.
2. Use WebGL for broad compatibility.
3. Build responsive HTML/CSS interface overlays.
4. Implement touch, mouse and keyboard controls.
5. Keep the game loop independent of any backend.
6. Use object pooling for road segments, coins, obstacles and reusable effects.
7. Use seeded procedural generation.
8. Persist local settings, currency and best score.
9. Keep systems modular and testable.
10. Provide a working development command and production build command.

## Gameplay requirements

Implement an explicit state machine with Escape, Theft and Coin Rush as mutually exclusive primary gameplay phases.

When Theft is active, suspend normal running obstacles and coin objectives. When Coin Rush is active, disable theft interactions and competing police threats. When Escape is active, focus gameplay on movement, obstacles and pursuit.

Maintain a shared run state so score, distance, coins, progression and pursuit pressure persist correctly across transitions.

Implement contextual gestures and clear visual prompts. Support mouse and keyboard equivalents for desktop players.

Theft success must unlock progression, while failure increases pursuit pressure or triggers a critical failure when appropriate.

## World requirements

Build a procedural city using compatible reusable segments. Maintain a bounded number of active segments. Ensure every mandatory transition has a valid route and every generated obstacle sequence is physically achievable.

Use a visible camera buffer ahead of the player. Remove or recycle segments after they are no longer needed.

## Police and gadgets

Implement escalating pursuit from foot officers to motorcycles, cars and helicopters. Use a configurable pursuit pressure system.

Gadgets must provide temporary arcade advantages with charges and cooldowns. They must not permanently eliminate the police or make the game impossible to lose.

## UI requirements

Implement a complete main menu, HUD, pause screen, settings screen, upgrade shop and animated game-over screen.

Use a dark, polished material-inspired interface with striped visual accents and metallic details. Keep the thief and police visually consistent with the supplied reference images.

## Quality requirements

- No dead buttons.
- No fake score updates.
- No duplicated rewards.
- No repeated phase-transition bugs.
- No unavoidable generated obstacle sequences.
- No infinite accumulation of world objects.
- No controls that require a mouse on mobile.
- No dependence on a developer console to play.
- No unhandled critical runtime errors.
- No misleading claims that an unimplemented feature is complete.

## Implementation workflow

Work in small, functional milestones.

First, inspect the available project files and establish the project structure. If the project is empty, scaffold it.

Then implement and test movement before adding the complete progression system. Add the three gameplay phases next, followed by theft encounters, vehicles, pursuit AI, procedural generation, gadgets, UI, persistence and optimisation.

After each milestone:
1. Run the development build.
2. Fix compilation and runtime errors.
3. Test the relevant gameplay.
4. Verify desktop and touch controls.
5. Check that existing functionality still works.
6. Document completed functionality and remaining limitations.

Do not replace working systems with mockups when moving to the next milestone.

If an asset is unavailable, create a visually consistent procedural or primitive placeholder and clearly document it. Do not block the core gameplay on the availability of paid assets.

## Final deliverables

Provide:
- Complete source code.
- Clear project structure.
- Asset and dependency instructions.
- Local setup instructions.
- Development and production commands.
- A production-ready web build.
- A concise list of completed features.
- A test checklist and known limitations.

Begin with the smallest playable version, then expand it into the complete game defined by this GDD.

**Final acceptance condition:** A player must be able to open the game in a browser, start a run, dodge obstacles, complete theft challenges, collect coins during separate bonus phases, experience escalating police pursuit, reach the balloon stage, get caught, view a final score, purchase upgrades and immediately play again.

The result must be an actual playable game, not merely a rendered scene or a collection of static UI screens.
